climate change is now happening so fast  that leaving oil in the ground is no  longer enough  a new assessment by scientists working  for the united nations shows we need to  halve our greenhouse gas emissions in  just eight years but we also need to  find some way of removing carbon dioxide  from the atmosphere to undo the damage  of the past  we are on a fast track to climate  disaster  it is underwater unprecedented it waves  terrifying storms widespread water  shortages the extinction of a million  species of plants and animals  and these is not fiction or exaggeration  it is what science tells us will result  from our current energy policies  nature can be our ally according to the  intergovernmental panel on climate  change steered marshes in somerset cover  just one square mile but they remove and  store almost 18 000 tons of carbon  dioxide from the atmosphere every year  scale that up plant trees stop peat bogs  being drained and rewilding could buy us  time  it can happen relatively quickly we know  how to do it  it takes time steer you know took eight  years or so but in the grand scheme of  things that's that's not a long time and  if we  unlock these sorts of solutions at scale  we can do this within the next five to  ten years and buy ourselves that time to  bring the technologies on stream in the  longer term to reduce our emissions  further  the first attempts are being made to use  technology to suck carbon dioxide out of  the air and store it underground it's  expensive and inefficient for now but  the un scientists say without it there  is little hope of raining in the rapid  rise in global temperatures  the glasgow climate conference was just  a small step in the right direction  pledges to cut emissions are not the  same as action and at the moment the  world is still on course for three  degrees of warming  the report is scathing about our  continued use of fossil fuels coal in  particular it calls for an even more  rapid shift to renewables more energy  efficient housing and a rethink of how  we design cities so people need to drive  less  one of the scientists says it can be  done  if we  act  now  if we act rapidly if we make the big  transformational changes that are  necessary  there's still a glimmer of hope of  getting to 1.5 and if we miss 1.5  we still stopped it going  1.6 1.7 1.8 so whatever effort we put in  will save lives  the report was delayed by two days as  195 governments wrangled over the text  the un chief has accused them of lying  over their past climate commitments and  scientists are clear on the urgent need  for action it's now or never thomas more  sky news
 

